#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Write the video of a talk into the page of the event that held it.

The PyBCN YouTube channel holds 89 videos. The export enumerates the
channel itself, its videos and streams tabs, and then its 14 playlists: a
video of a monthly meetup sits in the by-year playlist and in "PyBCN Meetup
Videos" as well, six videos sit in no playlist at all, and two unlisted
ones show in no tab and only a playlist reveals them. So this script reads
one normalized record per video and never a playlist or a tab count.

A video belongs to a talk and not to a day. Each one is written as a link of
type video on the talk record of the event front matter, the form the
thirteen large editions already use, which gives the talk page its Video
section (layouts/talks/single.html) and the row of the talk on the event
page its Watch link (layouts/_default/meetup.html, partials/agenda.html).
The player is on the talk page and on no other: a youtube-nocookie.com
frame, which sets no cookie until the reader presses play, but which still
asks Google for the player when the page loads, so Google sees the IP
address of the reader of that one page. An event page or a listing prints
the link and no frame, so that a page of 19 videos makes no request to
Google before a reader asks for one. The privacy policy of the site says
what the frame does.

Three recordings of the channel are of a whole session and not of one
talk: a block of lightning talks, the introduction of an evening, and the
live stream of a whole evening. No talk title can ever match them, and the
export says so, with attaches_to "meetup", "session_level" in flags, and
meetup_id naming the event, which the export picked out of the candidates
of that month by series (the monthly meetup, not the coding dojo or the
practice session held days earlier), or which a person declared (see
below). Such a video is written as an entry of an event-level
session_videos block, title and url, which partials/session_videos.html
prints under a Recordings heading after the talks. The script writes it
only when the
page meetup_id names exists and meetup_id is one of the candidates the
export considered; a record that fails either test is reported and left
out. The block is not called videos: Hugo's internal opengraph template
reads .Params.videos as a list of URL strings for og:video, and a list of
records under that key stops the build of the page.

Three records of the export are declared, not parsed. The title grammar of
the export reads the day, the talk, and the speaker out of a video title,
and three titles defeat it: a short name an uploader wrote for a long
title ("PySpark and Arrow"), a live stream with no date in its title, and
a live stream of a whole evening whose title names no talk. For each one a
person read the programme of its day and wrote the facts by id in
tools/parse_youtube.py of the pybcn-archive: the title the programme uses,
the event, whether the video is of one talk or of the whole session, and
the reason. The record then carries "declared" in flags and in match, and
declared_reason, with no candidates, because the person named the event
and the grammar never got to choose. The export is the right place for
that decision: it is where the raw title and the evidence sit side by side,
where a reader of this site can trace a published fact back to its source,
and a decision written there survives a rebuild of the export, which a
hand edit of a content file does not. This script treats a declared record
as what it is, evidence a human recorded: it looks the declared title up
on the one page the record names, by name and not by fuzzy match, and
writes the link on that talk, or the entry in session_videos when the
record attaches to the meetup. Every declared placement is reported in its
own group with its reason, the way the PROGRAMME group reports a video
placed on another day, so a human always sees them. A declared record that
names no page of this tree, or a title that names no talk of that page, is
reported and left out.

How a video finds its talk, and what stops a wrong match:

  the title   bin/titlematch.py, the rule bin/import-x-photos matches a
              talk title inside a tweet caption with, both_ways because the
              uploader cuts a long title and writes "[...]" where the cut
              is, so the YouTube title can be the shorter of the two. The
              length guard TITLE_MINIMUM is what keeps a short title from
              matching unrelated words.
  the day     the title of a video carries the day of the meetup, as a
              month ("PyBCN Meetup 2019/03") or as a full date. The talks
              of an event on that day are the first candidates. When none
              of them matches and exactly one talk of the whole archive
              does, the video goes on that talk, on the day of that other
              event: the month in a title is a label the uploader typed
              weeks after the day, and a title that matches one talk of
              the whole archive is a fact of the programme, so the
              programme wins. Measured on 2026-10-13, six of the 83 videos
              are placed that way, and each one is named in its own group
              of the report, with the day the title claims and the event
              that announced the talk, so a human always sees them. The
              risk this accepts: a talk given twice, where the archive
              recorded only one of the two occurrences, would take the
              video of the other one. No case of that exists in the current
              data. The uniqueness guard below is what keeps the rule from
              reaching further: a title that matches talks of two other
              days is still reported and not written.
  uniqueness  two candidate talks that match one video are reported and
              neither is written. A video on the wrong talk is worse than a
              video that is missing, because the page would state it and
              nothing on it would say the match was a guess.

Everything the run could not place with those facts is printed and left
out, with what is known about it: a video whose title matches no talk of
the day, a video whose day holds no talk record at all, a video with no day
in its title, and a session recording whose event the export does not
name or this tree does not hold. A human decides each one.

One record of the export is not a failure of the matching. The channel
holds a presentation of the Barcelona community given at PyCon ES 2020,
which is not a talk of any PyBCN event, and the export marks it as such:
"out_of_scope" in flags, with an out_of_scope_reason a person wrote. The
run reports it on its own line, with that reason, and places it nowhere.
That is correct and expected, not an error, so it does not fail the run.

Exit codes:
    0  every video was placed, or was already in place
    1  a video could not be placed, or a page could not be written
    2  the script could not run: bad arguments, or a missing file

Usage:
    bin/import-videos VIDEOS.json [--out content/events] [--dry-run]

VIDEOS.json is data/normalized/videos.json of the pybcn-archive export,
which is not in this repository. One record per video, with the title of the
talk, the day read from the video title, the URL, and the id of the video,
which the report names it by and which falls back to the URL.
"""

import argparse
import datetime
import json
import pathlib
import re
import sys

import yaml

# canon.py and titlematch.py sit next to this script and are not installed
# packages.
sys.path.insert(0, str(pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent))

from canon import canon  # noqa: E402
from titlematch import fold, match_title  # noqa: E402

ROOT = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent

# The default location of the event pages, so the usual run needs no extra
# argument.
EVENTS_DIR = ROOT / "content" / "events"

# The YAML front matter of a Hugo page, the same shape bin/check-content and
# bin/import-x-photos read: the first block between two "---" lines.
FRONT_MATTER = re.compile(r"\A---\r?\n(.*?)\r?\n---[ \t]*(?:\r?\n|\Z)", re.S)

# The talks block is written in two indentations. The thirteen large
# editions and 189 meetup pages put the dash of a record two spaces in from
# the "talks:" key, a field four in, the dash of a link six, and a field of
# the link eight. The 35 pages that a YAML dump wrote put the dash of a
# record under the key, so a field sits two in, the dash of a link two as
# well, and a field of the link four. Both parse to the same records, so
# the offset of a dash from its key is read from the first record of the
# block and the link is written the same way. A guess that comes out wrong
# is caught before the write, because the edited text has to parse back to
# the same front matter plus one link (see unchanged_apart_from).
TALKS_KEY = re.compile(r"^talks:[ \t]*$")
RECORD_LINE = re.compile(r"^( *)- id: (.*?)[ \t]*$")

# The event-level session_videos block, for a recording of a whole session.
# Written in the indentation of the large editions, two spaces to the dash,
# four to a field, whatever the talks block of the same page uses: the two
# are separate blocks and YAML reads each one by its own indentation. The
# key is not "videos", which Hugo reserves for og:video (see the docstring).
VIDEOS_KEY = re.compile(r"^session_videos:[ \t]*$")
VIDEOS_COMMENT = [
    "# A recording of a whole session of this meetup and not of one talk: a",
    "# block of lightning talks, or the introduction of the evening, has no",
    "# talk record for a video to sit on, so the video sits on the event.",
    "# bin/import-videos writes this block from the YouTube export of the",
    "# pybcn-archive: a correction goes to the export or to the script, never",
    "# here. The page prints these under a Recordings heading, after the talks",
    "# (partials/session_videos.html). Not \"videos\": Hugo reads that key",
    "# as a list of og:video URLs, and a list of records there stops the build.",
]
VIDEO_TITLE = "  - title: %s"
VIDEO_URL = '    url: "%s"'


def fail(message):
    """Stop the run with exit code 2: the script cannot do its job."""
    sys.exit("import-videos: %s" % message)


def plural(count, noun):
    """Return "1 video" or "4 videos": a count a person reads out loud."""
    return "%d %s" % (count, noun if count == 1 else noun + "s")


def read_events(events_dir):
    """Return one record per event page, with the talks of each.

    A page with no front matter, and a talk with no id or no title, is
    skipped: bin/check-content is what reports those, and this script only
    needs the records it can address.
    """
    events = []
    for path in sorted(events_dir.rglob("*.md")):
        match = FRONT_MATTER.match(path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
        if not match:
            continue
        try:
            data = yaml.safe_load(match.group(1)) or {}
        except yaml.YAMLError as error:
            fail("cannot parse the front matter of %s: %s" % (path, error))
        day = data.get("date")
        if isinstance(day, datetime.datetime):
            day = day.date()
        if not isinstance(day, datetime.date):
            day = None
        talks, held = [], {}
        for record in data.get("talks") or []:
            if not isinstance(record, dict):
                continue
            talk_id, title = record.get("id"), record.get("title")
            if not talk_id or not title:
                continue
            videos = videos_of(record)
            held.update({url: "talk '%s'" % talk_id for url in videos})
            talks.append({"id": talk_id, "title": title,
                          "folded": fold(title), "videos": videos})
        # A session with no talk record of its own carries its video on the
        # row of the grid, which is where the welcome of PyDay BCN 2020
        # holds one (see partials/agenda_entry.html). No video is ever
        # written there by this script, because a monthly meetup has no
        # grid, but a video already in place has to be recognised wherever
        # it sits or a second copy of it would be written.
        for slot in data.get("agenda") or []:
            if not isinstance(slot, dict) or slot.get("talk"):
                continue
            label = slot.get("label") or slot.get("type") or "a row of the grid"
            held.update({url: "the grid row '%s'" % label for url in videos_of(slot)})
        # The recording of a whole session, which this script writes on the
        # event (see the docstring), has to be recognised on a second run.
        for entry in data.get("session_videos") or []:
            if isinstance(entry, dict) and entry.get("url"):
                held[entry["url"]] = "the session_videos block of the event"
        events.append({"path": path, "name": path.stem, "day": day,
                       "talks": talks, "held": held})
    return events


def videos_of(record):
    """Return the video URLs of one talk or one row of the grid."""
    return [link.get("url") for link in record.get("links") or []
            if isinstance(link, dict) and link.get("type") == "video"]


def day_agrees(video, day):
    """Return True when an event day is the day the video title names.

    A full date in the title has to be that date. A month has to be that
    month. A video whose title carries no day at all is matched on the year
    alone, which is the only fact left, and the uniqueness guard is then
    what decides whether it can be placed.
    """
    if day is None:
        return False
    if video.get("date"):
        return str(day) == video["date"]
    if video.get("year") and video.get("month"):
        return day.year == video["year"] and day.month == video["month"]
    if video.get("year"):
        return day.year == video["year"]
    return False


def candidates_of(events, video, on_the_day):
    """Return the talks to match one video against, as titlematch pairs.

    on_the_day picks the two uses: the talks of the day the title names,
    which is where a video may be placed, and the talks of every event,
    which is how a title that matches another day gets reported instead of
    silently lost.
    """
    pairs, where = [], {}
    for event in events:
        if on_the_day and not day_agrees(video, event["day"]):
            continue
        for talk in event["talks"]:
            key = (event["name"], talk["id"])
            pairs.append((key, talk["folded"]))
            where[key] = (event, talk)
    return pairs, where


def classify(video, events):
    """Return (state, detail) for one video.

    The states, and what each one means for the page:

      placed        one talk of the day matches, and the page gets the link
      already       a page already carries this URL, so nothing is written
      other_video   the matched talk carries another video, for a human to
                    compare
      ambiguous     two talks of the day match, so neither is written
      off_day       no talk of the day matches, but exactly one talk of
                    another day does, so the programme wins over the label
                    in the title and the page of that other day gets the
                    link
      other_day     no talk of the day matches, and talks of two other days
                    do, so the programme cannot say which one and neither
                    is written
      no_talk       no talk anywhere matches this title
      out_of_scope  the export says this video belongs to no PyBCN event,
                    so it is never placed, and that is not an error
      session       a recording of a whole session, which the export
                    attaches to an event by meetup_id, and that event page
                    gets an entry of its session_videos block
      no_session    a recording of a whole session whose meetup_id is
                    missing, is not one of the candidates of the export,
                    or names no page of this tree, so nothing is written
      declared      a person declared the placement in the export, by id
                    and with a reason, and the page the record names
                    holds the talk it names, or gets an entry of its
                    session_videos block when the record attaches to the
                    meetup
      no_declared   a declared record that names no page of this tree, or
                    a title that names no talk of that page, so nothing is
                    written
    """
    # A URL already on a page is in place, wherever on that page it sits.
    # This is what makes a second run of the script write nothing, and what
    # keeps the twenty videos of PyDay BCN 2020 where a human put them.
    for event in events:
        if video["url"] in event["held"]:
            return "already", (event, event["held"][video["url"]])

    flags = video.get("flags") or []
    if "out_of_scope" in flags:
        return "out_of_scope", video.get("out_of_scope_reason") or "no reason given"
    if "declared" in flags:
        return classify_declared(video, events)
    if "session_level" in flags and video.get("attaches_to") == "meetup":
        return classify_session(video, events)

    title = video.get("talk_title") or ""
    pairs, where = candidates_of(events, video, on_the_day=True)
    key, hits = match_title(title, pairs, both_ways=True)
    if key:
        event, talk = where[key]
        if talk["videos"]:
            return "other_video", (event, talk)
        return "placed", (event, talk)
    if hits:
        return "ambiguous", [where[hit] for hit in hits]

    pairs, where = candidates_of(events, video, on_the_day=False)
    key, hits = match_title(title, pairs, both_ways=True)
    if key:
        event, talk = where[key]
        if talk["videos"]:
            return "other_video", (event, talk)
        return "off_day", (event, talk)
    if hits:
        return "other_day", [where[hit] for hit in hits]
    return "no_talk", [event for event in events if day_agrees(video, event["day"])]


def classify_session(video, events):
    """Return (state, detail) for the recording of a whole session.

    The export names the event in meetup_id, chosen among the candidates of
    the month by series. Three facts have to hold before anything is
    written: meetup_id is there, it is one of those candidates (so the
    export did not name an event it never considered), and this tree holds
    a page of that name whose day agrees with the title. The first two are
    read from the export, the third from the pages.
    """
    meetup_id = video.get("meetup_id")
    if not meetup_id:
        return "no_session", "the export names no meetup_id"
    candidates = video.get("candidates") or []
    if meetup_id not in candidates:
        return "no_session", ("meetup_id %r is not one of the candidates of the "
                              "export: %s" % (meetup_id, ", ".join(candidates) or "none"))
    event = next((event for event in events if event["name"] == meetup_id), None)
    if event is None:
        return "no_session", "no page of content/events is named %r" % meetup_id
    if not day_agrees(video, event["day"]):
        return "no_session", ("the page %s is dated %s, and the title says %s"
                              % (meetup_id, event["day"], label_of(video)))
    return "session", event


def classify_declared(video, events):
    """Return (state, detail) for a record a person declared in the export.

    The declaration names the event in meetup_id and the talk by the title
    the programme uses (see the docstring). The record has no candidates,
    because the person chose and the grammar did not, so the tests are:
    meetup_id names a page of this tree, the day of that page agrees with
    the record, and, for a video of one talk, exactly one talk of that page
    carries the declared title. The title is compared folded, the way the
    archive compares every title, but never matched partially: a declared
    title is the name of the talk, not a label an uploader cut short. The
    detail is (event, talk), with talk None for a recording of the whole
    session, which goes in session_videos.
    """
    meetup_id = video.get("meetup_id")
    if not meetup_id:
        return "no_declared", "the declared record names no meetup_id"
    event = next((event for event in events if event["name"] == meetup_id), None)
    if event is None:
        return "no_declared", "no page of content/events is named %r" % meetup_id
    if not day_agrees(video, event["day"]):
        return "no_declared", ("the page %s is dated %s, and the record says %s"
                               % (meetup_id, event["day"], label_of(video)))
    if video.get("attaches_to") == "meetup":
        return "declared", (event, None)
    folded = fold(video["talk_title"])
    found = [talk for talk in event["talks"] if talk["folded"] == folded]
    if not found:
        return "no_declared", ("no talk of %s is titled %r"
                               % (meetup_id, video["talk_title"]))
    if len(found) > 1:
        return "no_declared", ("%d talks of %s are titled %r: %s"
                               % (len(found), meetup_id, video["talk_title"],
                                  ", ".join(talk["id"] for talk in found)))
    if found[0]["videos"]:
        return "other_video", (event, found[0])
    return "declared", (event, found[0])


def record_bounds(lines, close, talk_id):
    """Return the first line, the end line, and the dash offset of one talk.

    The record starts at its "- id:" line and runs to the line before the
    next record of the block, or before the end of the front matter. Every
    field of a record, and every item of a list inside it, is indented
    further than the dash, which is what ends the scan. The offset is the
    indentation of the dash from the "talks:" key, 0 or 2, which is also the
    indentation of the dash of a link from its "links:" key in the same
    file.
    """
    try:
        opened = next(i for i in range(close) if TALKS_KEY.match(lines[i]))
    except StopIteration:
        return None, None, None
    offset, start = None, None
    for position in range(opened + 1, close):
        found = RECORD_LINE.match(lines[position])
        if not found:
            continue
        if offset is None:
            offset = len(found.group(1))
        if len(found.group(1)) != offset:
            break  # a list of another block, deeper or shallower
        if found.group(2).strip("'\"") == talk_id:
            start = position
            break
        if not lines[position].startswith(" " * offset):
            break
    if start is None:
        return None, None, None
    field = " " * (offset + 2)
    end = start + 1
    while end < close and lines[end].startswith(field):
        end += 1
    return start, end, offset


def insert_video(text, talk_id, url):
    """Return the page text with a video link on one talk, or None.

    The link goes first in the list, which is the order the thirteen large
    editions write (video, then slides), and a list that is not there yet is
    written at the end of the record. None means the record was not found,
    which the caller reports rather than writing a page it did not
    understand.
    """
    lines = text.split("\n")
    if not lines or lines[0].strip() != "---":
        return None
    try:
        close = lines.index("---", 1)
    except ValueError:
        return None

    start, end, offset = record_bounds(lines, close, talk_id)
    if start is None:
        return None

    field = " " * (offset + 2)
    links = field + "links:"
    link = [field + " " * offset + "- type: video",
            field + " " * offset + '  url: "%s"' % url]
    for position in range(start, end):
        if lines[position] == links:
            return "\n".join(lines[:position + 1] + link + lines[position + 1:])
    return "\n".join(lines[:end] + [links] + link + lines[end:])


def insert_session_video(text, title, url):
    """Return the page text with one entry in the event-level session_videos
    block.

    A block already there gets the entry at its end, after the entries it
    holds. A page without one gets the block at the end of the front
    matter, under the comment that says what it is. None means the front
    matter was not found, which the caller reports rather than writing a
    page it did not understand.
    """
    lines = text.split("\n")
    if not lines or lines[0].strip() != "---":
        return None
    try:
        close = lines.index("---", 1)
    except ValueError:
        return None
    entry = [VIDEO_TITLE % json.dumps(title, ensure_ascii=False), VIDEO_URL % url]
    opened = next((i for i in range(1, close) if VIDEOS_KEY.match(lines[i])), None)
    if opened is None:
        return "\n".join(lines[:close] + VIDEOS_COMMENT + ["session_videos:"] + entry + lines[close:])
    end = opened + 1
    while end < close and lines[end].startswith("  "):
        end += 1
    return "\n".join(lines[:end] + entry + lines[end:])


def unchanged_apart_from_session(before, after, title, url):
    """Return True when the only difference between two front matters is one
    more entry at the end of the event-level session_videos block."""
    if {k: v for k, v in (before or {}).items() if k != "session_videos"} \
            != {k: v for k, v in (after or {}).items() if k != "session_videos"}:
        return False
    was = (before or {}).get("session_videos") or []
    return (after or {}).get("session_videos") == was + [{"title": title, "url": url}]


def write_session(video, event, dry_run):
    """Put the recording of a whole session on its event. Return None, or
    what went wrong."""
    path = event["path"]
    title = video["talk_title"] or video.get("title_raw") or video["video_id"]
    text = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    updated = insert_session_video(text, title, video["url"])
    if updated is None:
        return "no front matter to write the session_videos block into"
    if not unchanged_apart_from_session(front_matter_of(text), front_matter_of(updated),
                                        title, video["url"]):
        return "the edit would change more than the session_videos block"
    if not dry_run:
        path.write_text(updated, encoding="utf-8")
        data = front_matter_of(path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")) or {}
        urls = [entry.get("url") for entry in data.get("session_videos") or []
                if isinstance(entry, dict)]
        if video["url"] not in urls:
            return "the page does not carry the URL after the write"
    event["held"][video["url"]] = "the session_videos block of the event"
    return None


def written_talk(path, talk_id):
    """Return the talk record of a page as the front matter now parses it.

    The page is read again from disk, so what is reported is what the file
    holds and not what this script meant to write.
    """
    match = FRONT_MATTER.match(path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    if not match:
        return None
    data = yaml.safe_load(match.group(1)) or {}
    for record in data.get("talks") or []:
        if isinstance(record, dict) and record.get("id") == talk_id:
            return record
    return None


def unchanged_apart_from(before, after, talk_id, url):
    """Return True when the only difference between two front matters is the
    video link on one talk.

    The page is written as text, so this is the check that the text edit did
    what the record says: everything else of the front matter, every other
    talk, and every other field of this talk have to come back equal, and
    this talk has to carry the URL as the first link of type video.
    """
    before_talks = (before or {}).get("talks") or []
    after_talks = (after or {}).get("talks") or []
    if len(before_talks) != len(after_talks):
        return False
    if {k: v for k, v in (before or {}).items() if k != "talks"} \
            != {k: v for k, v in (after or {}).items() if k != "talks"}:
        return False
    for was, now in zip(before_talks, after_talks):
        if not isinstance(was, dict) or not isinstance(now, dict):
            return False
        if was.get("id") != talk_id:
            if was != now:
                return False
            continue
        if {k: v for k, v in was.items() if k != "links"} \
                != {k: v for k, v in now.items() if k != "links"}:
            return False
        links = now.get("links") or []
        if links[:1] != [{"type": "video", "url": url}]:
            return False
        if links[1:] != (was.get("links") or []):
            return False
    return True


def front_matter_of(text):
    """Return the parsed front matter of a page text, or None."""
    match = FRONT_MATTER.match(text)
    if not match:
        return None
    return yaml.safe_load(match.group(1)) or {}


def write(video, event, talk, dry_run):
    """Put one video on one talk. Return None, or what went wrong."""
    path = event["path"]
    text = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    updated = insert_video(text, talk["id"], video["url"])
    if updated is None:
        return "no record '%s' in the talks block" % talk["id"]
    if not unchanged_apart_from(front_matter_of(text), front_matter_of(updated),
                                talk["id"], video["url"]):
        return ("the edit would change more than the links of '%s'"
                % talk["id"])
    if not dry_run:
        path.write_text(updated, encoding="utf-8")
        record = written_talk(path, talk["id"])
        urls = [link.get("url") for link in (record or {}).get("links") or []
                if isinstance(link, dict) and link.get("type") == "video"]
        if video["url"] not in urls:
            return "the page does not carry the URL after the write"
    talk["videos"] = [video["url"]] + talk["videos"]
    return None


def label_of(video):
    """Return the day a video title names, as it reads in a report."""
    if video.get("date"):
        return video["date"]
    if video.get("year") and video.get("month"):
        return "%04d-%02d" % (video["year"], video["month"])
    if video.get("year"):
        return str(video["year"])
    return "no day in the title"


def report(groups, dry_run):
    """Print what the run did and what it left for a human."""
    placed = groups["placed"]
    off_day = groups["off_day"]
    already = groups["already"]
    pages = sorted({event["path"].name for video, event, talk in placed})
    talks = {(event["name"], talk["id"]) for video, event, talk in placed}

    print()
    print("%s on %s, over %s"
          % (plural(len(placed), "video"), plural(len(talks), "talk"),
             plural(len(pages), "page")))
    # Counted apart from the line above on purpose: a video placed on a day
    # its title does not name is a decision the programme took over the
    # label, and a human has to be able to see every one of them.
    print("%s placed where the programme announced the talk, "
          "not on the day the title says" % plural(len(off_day), "video"))
    print("%s written on the event as the recording of a whole session"
          % plural(len(groups["session"]), "video"))
    # Counted apart as well: a declared placement is a decision a person
    # wrote in the export, not a match the grammar found, and a human has to
    # be able to see every one of them with its reason.
    print("%s placed as a person declared in the export, by id and with a reason"
          % plural(len(groups["declared"]), "video"))
    print("%s already in place and not touched" % plural(len(already), "video"))
    for video, (event, where) in sorted(already, key=lambda row: row[1][0]["name"]):
        print("  in place %-12s %-62s -> %s, on %s"
              % (video["video_id"], video["talk_title"][:62], event["name"], where))
    for state, heading in (
            ("ambiguous", "left out, two talks of the day match one video"),
            ("other_video", "left out, the talk already carries another video"),
            ("other_day", "left out, the day in the title and the programme disagree"),
            ("no_talk", "left out, no talk of the archive carries this title"),
            ("no_session", "left out, a session recording with no page to go to"),
            ("no_declared", "left out, a declared record that names no page or no talk of this tree"),
            ("failed", "left out, the page could not be written"),
            ("out_of_scope", "out of scope, which the export says and is not an error")):
        group = groups[state]
        print("%s %s" % (plural(len(group), "video"), heading))

    for video, event, talk in sorted(placed, key=lambda row: row[1]["name"]):
        print("  placed  %-12s %-62s -> %s / %s"
              % (video["video_id"], video["talk_title"][:62], event["name"], talk["id"]))
    for video, event, talk in sorted(off_day, key=lambda row: row[1]["name"]):
        print("  PROGRAMME %-12s title says %s, %r is announced by %s / %s"
              % (video["video_id"], label_of(video), video["talk_title"],
                 event["name"], talk["id"]))
    for video, event in sorted(groups["session"], key=lambda row: row[1]["name"]):
        print("  SESSION %-12s %r -> %s, in the session_videos block of the event"
              % (video["video_id"], video["talk_title"], event["name"]))
    for video, event, talk in sorted(groups["declared"], key=lambda row: row[1]["name"]):
        where = ("%s / %s" % (event["name"], talk["id"]) if talk
                 else "%s, in the session_videos block of the event" % event["name"])
        print("  DECLARED %-12s %r -> %s: %s"
              % (video["video_id"], video["talk_title"], where,
                 video.get("declared_reason") or "no reason given"))
    for video, found in groups["ambiguous"]:
        print("  AMBIGUOUS %-12s %r matches %s"
              % (video["video_id"], video["talk_title"],
                 ", ".join("%s / %s" % (event["name"], talk["id"]) for event, talk in found)))
    for video, (event, talk) in groups["other_video"]:
        print("  OTHER VIDEO %-12s %s / %s already carries %s"
              % (video["video_id"], event["name"], talk["id"],
                 ", ".join(talk["videos"])))
    for video, found in groups["other_day"]:
        print("  OTHER DAY %-12s title says %s, %r is announced by %s"
              % (video["video_id"], label_of(video), video["talk_title"],
                 ", ".join("%s / %s" % (event["name"], talk["id"]) for event, talk in found)))
    for video, found in groups["no_talk"]:
        where = ", ".join(event["name"] for event in found) or "no event of that day"
        print("  NO TALK %-12s title says %s, %r, day holds: %s"
              % (video["video_id"], label_of(video), video["talk_title"], where))
    for video, why in groups["no_session"]:
        print("  NO SESSION PAGE %-12s title says %s, %r: %s"
              % (video["video_id"], label_of(video), video["talk_title"], why))
    for video, why in groups["no_declared"]:
        print("  NO DECLARED PAGE %-12s record says %s, %r: %s"
              % (video["video_id"], label_of(video), video["talk_title"], why))
    for video, why in groups["failed"]:
        print("  FAILED  %-12s %s" % (video["video_id"], why))
    for video, why in groups["out_of_scope"]:
        print("  OUT OF SCOPE %-12s %r: %s" % (video["video_id"], video["talk_title"], why))

    if dry_run:
        print("\n--dry-run: no page was written.")


def main():
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        description="Write the video of a talk into the page of its event.")
    parser.add_argument("videos", type=pathlib.Path,
                        help="the normalized videos.json of the archive export")
    parser.add_argument("--out", type=pathlib.Path, default=EVENTS_DIR,
                        help="the directory of the event pages "
                             "(default: content/events)")
    parser.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true",
                        help="report what would be written, and write nothing")
    args = parser.parse_args()

    if not args.videos.is_file():
        fail("no such file: %s" % args.videos)
    if not args.out.is_dir():
        fail("no such directory: %s" % args.out)

    try:
        videos = json.loads(args.videos.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except (OSError, ValueError) as error:
        fail("cannot read %s: %s" % (args.videos, error))
    if not isinstance(videos, list):
        fail("%s does not hold a list of videos" % args.videos)

    seen, records = set(), []
    for video in videos:
        if not isinstance(video, dict) or not video.get("url"):
            fail("a record of %s has no url" % args.videos)
        # An address of this archive is https. bin/check-rendered fails a
        # built page that carries an http:// URL, and a video link reaches
        # the page as written.
        if not video["url"].startswith("https://"):
            fail("video %s has the URL %r, which is not https"
                 % (video.get("video_id"), video["url"]))
        # The playlists overlap, so the export is read by video id and a
        # repeated id would write one link twice.
        video_id = video.get("video_id") or video["url"]
        if video_id in seen:
            fail("video %s appears twice in %s" % (video_id, args.videos))
        seen.add(video_id)
        # The id is written back, because the report names every video by it
        # and a record without one would end the run with a KeyError after
        # the pages were already written.
        video["video_id"] = video_id
        video["talk_title"] = canon(video.get("talk_title") or "").strip()
        records.append(video)

    events = read_events(args.out)
    groups = {state: [] for state in ("placed", "off_day", "already",
                                      "session", "declared", "ambiguous",
                                      "other_video", "other_day", "no_talk",
                                      "no_session", "no_declared", "failed",
                                      "out_of_scope")}
    for video in records:
        state, detail = classify(video, events)
        if state in ("placed", "off_day"):
            event, talk = detail
            problem = write(video, event, talk, args.dry_run)
            if problem:
                groups["failed"].append((video, problem))
            else:
                groups[state].append((video, event, talk))
        elif state == "declared":
            event, talk = detail
            if talk:
                problem = write(video, event, talk, args.dry_run)
            else:
                problem = write_session(video, event, args.dry_run)
            if problem:
                groups["failed"].append((video, problem))
            else:
                groups["declared"].append((video, event, talk))
        elif state == "session":
            problem = write_session(video, detail, args.dry_run)
            if problem:
                groups["failed"].append((video, problem))
            else:
                groups["session"].append((video, detail))
        elif state == "already":
            groups["already"].append((video, detail))
        elif state == "other_video":
            groups["other_video"].append((video, detail))
        else:
            groups[state].append((video, detail))

    report(groups, args.dry_run)
    left = sum(len(groups[state]) for state in
               ("ambiguous", "other_video", "other_day", "no_talk",
                "no_session", "no_declared", "failed"))
    return 1 if left else 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
